enclave shared-base mirror: one-load heap base for defined shared memories
==========================================================================

THE COST: a DEFINED shared memory's VMMemoryDefinition lives out-of-line
(an Arc the SharedMemory owns), so compiled code reached the heap base by
chasing vmctx -> *mut VMMemoryDefinition -> base: two dependent loads,
where a plain (owned) memory takes one. Both loads are readonly and hoist
out of call-free loops — but every CALL forces a rederivation, and an
interpreter-shaped guest calls in its per-instruction path. Measured on a
call-per-iteration microbench (callee does 3 loads, call_indirect so it
cannot inline): plain 0.780s, shared 0.833s = +6.4%. On risc-box (DOOM
demo, guest MIPS): the same mechanism is most of the gap that remained
after -W set-epochs=n.

THE FIX: a new per-defined-memory vmctx area, `defined_shared_memory_bases`,
holding a copy of the base pointer, written at instantiation. Sound because
a shared memory's base can NEVER move (allocated at its max reservation;
grow only mprotects). Codegen (make_heap_base_bound, defined+shared) takes
the base from that mirror — ONE readonly load, byte-identical shape to an
owned memory — while the LENGTH still goes through the out-of-line
definition, because concurrent growth must stay visible across instances
(with bounds-check elision on, loads/stores never read it anyway; memory.size
and the slow paths still do, through the unchanged pointer).

THE TWO WRITERS, and why both matter:
  - initialize_vmctx: writes the mirror beside the definition pointer for
    every defined shared memory (and initializes the dead slot for owned
    memories so no vmctx bytes are undefined — dead because compiled code
    only reads the mirror for `shared` memories, whose base is immutable).
  - replace_defined_memory_with_shared (the SET worker re-point): a worker
    view re-pointed at the primary's SharedMemory MUST get the primary's
    base mirrored, or the worker would run on its own discarded memory.
    Runs before the view executes any guest code, same as the pointer write.

VERIFIED: shared/unshared minimal pair objdump — load sites now identical
(movq 0xNN(%rdi),%rsi; access) both ways; callloop microbench 0.780 vs
0.781s (parity, was +6.4%); risc-box SET 121.7 MIPS vs plain 129.2 on the
same rig (was 105.8 under the epoch-armed engine two patches ago); SET
probes spawn-indirect=7, parallel(8)=8, stress(50x8)=400, nested=1, spin/
park teardown probes correct in both epoch modes; tests/all 1305/0.

Applies LAST, after wasmtime-set-epochs (diffed against the fully-patched
tree).

ADVERSARIAL REVIEW ROUND (2026-08-22, two independent reviewers): the
runtime/codegen soundness survived every attack (single lockstep writer
pair, immutable shared base with a RELEASE-mode assert upstream, write-
before-execute ordering incl. the SET worker re-point, index discipline,
winch/pulley staying on the still-correct two-load path). ONE confirmed
latent hazard, fixed in this revision: this is the first fleet patch to
CHANGE VMCTX LAYOUT, and upstream's artifact fence is only the MAJOR
version ("49") — a pre-mirror .cwasm, or a ~/.cache/wasmtime entry (the
CLI caches by default), would deserialize cleanly and index every field
after owned_memories at stale offsets: calls through garbage pointers.
So the patch now also stamps ENCLAVE_VMCTX_LAYOUT_EPOCH ("49+enclave-
vmctx1") into the serialized version string AND the cache-key hash
(config.rs) — stale artifacts are refused loudly in both directions
("Module was compiled with incompatible version '49'", verified), costing
one recompile instead of memory corruption. BUMP THE EPOCH STRING ON EVERY
FUTURE PATCH THAT CHANGES VMCTX LAYOUT.

